That's certainly a recurring theme I see when, from time to time, I
read threads like this. Generally the word "Gehenna" comes up. :-)
Never been there, but it does sound rather boring.

Really, there are two fundamentally different kinds of challenges in
roguelikes that often unjustly get mentioned alongside each other:
those that reward long, systematic preparation, the "persistence"
mentioned in a post below -- a grind -- and those that reward choosing
repeatedly the right tactics and (non-grind-based) strategy, where the
range of these that enable these is narrow enough to be tough and broad
enough not to be the same each time. A very hard type of challenge to
devise, but rewarding for the player indeed. I will go out on a limb
here and call the latter type "better."

(I get the feeling that conducts fall into both groups: some forbid
certain useful non-grindish reactions, and can be overcome by grinding
(e.g. wishless), and some forbid a certain type of grind (e.g.
polypileless); put several together, and you either have a grind-free
game... or one that causes even more time spent on other types of
grinding, perhaps? Yet they're all called "conducts"...)

>   Gehennom gets mentioned a lot in this context.  Players think
> its too long and boring.  They want a more interesting challenge
> instead.

As long as grinding can be used, any challenge can be met by more
grinding; any challenge is a test of persistence.

Crawl generally makes grinding impossible by neither making it possible
to keep satiated for an extended time on cleared levels without dipping
into non-renewable food supplies nor providing any reasonably early
"infinite" areas a la Angband or ADOM (but, laudably IMO, not Nethack).
Thus you go into any challenges you choose within it (and those
required for ascension) based on average-goodies-per-level *
luck-of-the-draw * levels-reasonably-doable-before-challenge.

It doesn't seem to me -- again as a casual observer -- that that's
really an option for Nethack, as fundamentally changing the nature of
the food clock would just *not* be cool; it just wouldn't be Nethack
anymore, right? But doesn't Nethack have some other, quite successful
clocks, at the very least the post-Rodney clock? So it does have a
tradition of non-food clocks, and maybe Gehenna could be replaced with
an area with a newly invented non-food clock (and some measures to
negate the benefit of grinding in preparation for said challenge, e.g.
some sort of hard ("you can't") or soft ("you can -- at your own risk,
muahaha") inventory restrictions, etc.).

